# Vendor firmware this image does not carry, and the board supplies.
#
# One line per file:
#   <known-good sha256 or ->  <expected size>  <destination>  [optional]
#
# Neither column is a gate. The vendor reissues these files: a rebuild with a
# minor change is a different checksum and a slightly different size while
# being the same firmware, and refusing to import it would strand a board that
# is perfectly capable of running. So:
#
#   sha256  a file known to be right. If a candidate matches it, it is taken
#           at once and nothing else is considered. "-" means none is known.
#   size    what the file is expected to weigh, used only to tell same-named
#           files apart. A candidate is accepted when it lands between half
#           and double this, and the closest one wins.
#
# The size bound is not pedantry. /lib/firmware/video/video_ucode.bin on the
# device is a symlink to the 389,696-byte T7 codec firmware, which the VideoFW
# TA rejects at its RSA check (0xffff3072); the file that belongs here is
# Android's signed 1,951,232-byte package. Two different files, one name --
# matching on the name alone would import the one that cannot work.
#
# "optional" marks a file that not every firmware revision carries and that
# nothing in this image depends on. It is imported when the board has it; when
# it does not, that is not a failure and does not keep the import running on
# every boot. 9a04f079-....ta is one: no Chiron component opens that TA, and
# the Android 11 build of October 2023 has six TAs in vendor/lib/teetz, not
# seven -- checked on a board.
#
# No path inside the Android tree is named. chiron-firmware-import searches the
# read-only Android volumes by name; the vendor's own layout is not ours to
# depend on.
#
# These files are all third-party: Himax (touch controller firmware), Amlogic
# (signed trusted applications, the video microcode and the Bluetooth
# firmware) and the appliance vendor's amplifier tuning for the two FS2105
# parts, written with FourSemi's tool. This project has no redistribution
# grant for any of them and ships none of them. What it ships is this list; the bytes come off the
# board's own storage. See THIRD_PARTY.md.
#
# The Zigbee coordinator's own firmware is deliberately NOT here: it lives on
# the Telink coordinator chip, the host only talks to it over /dev/ttyS3, and
# nothing in this image loads or reflashes it.
